<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pusiga</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pusiga is a town in the Upper East Region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>, near the borders with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/togo">Togo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burkina-faso">Burkina Faso</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mole-dagbani">Mole-Dagbani</a> tradition names it as the home of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/na-gbewaa">Na Gbewaa</a>, the ancestor of the ruling houses of Mamprugu, Dagbon, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanumba">Nanumba</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mossi">Mossi</a> kingdoms, and his shrine there is still honoured.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Driven south by reprisals from the rising Songhay Empire, Kpoginumbo seized power at Biun in Gurma, and his son Naa Gbewaa left Biun to settle at <b>Pusiga</b> in the north-east corner of Ghana.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-dagbon">Kingdom of Dagbon · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Mole-Dagbani tradition traces Mamprugu to Na Gbewaa, a chief who settled at <b>Pusiga</b> in the far north of present-day Ghana, probably in the 14th or 15th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/mamprugu">Mamprugu</a></li></ul></section>
